\b Big Ass Fans Haiku (SenseME) - Unofficial Control4 Driver\b0\par
\par
This is a community-built driver, not provided or certified by Big Ass Fans or Control4.\par
It talks to the fan directly over the local network using the SenseME UDP protocol\par
(broadcast/unicast ASCII commands on UDP port 31415). No cloud connection is required or used.\par
\par
\b Setup\b0\par
1. Ensure the fan and the Control4 controller are on the same subnet/VLAN and that UDP 31415 is not blocked.\par
2. Give the fan a static/reserved IP (DHCP reservation) and enter it in the "Fan IP Address" property - this is required. \par
3. Run the "Rediscover Fan" action/command to broadcast a discovery request; the driver will auto-populate the "Fan Name (SenseME App Name)" and "Fan MAC Address" properties from the fan's reply. (Note: the reply's source IP is not available to this driver, so IP address itself must still be entered manually - see step 2.)\par
4. If the fan does not have a light kit, set "Has Light Kit" to No to hide/ignore the light proxy behavior.\par
5. "Max Fan Speed (0-7)" should match your fan model's top discrete speed step (most Haiku fans are 0-7).\par
\par
\b Properties\b0\par
- Fan IP Address: static/reserved IP of the fan on your LAN (recommended: DHCP reservation).\par
- Fan Name (SenseME App Name): the exact name configured in the SenseME app; used as the addressing token in every command.\par
- Fan MAC Address: read-only, populated by discovery.\par
- Has Light Kit: enables/disables the light proxy behavior.\par
- Max Fan Speed (0-7): used to translate Control4's 0-100% speed/level to the fan's discrete steps.\par
- Poll Interval (seconds): how often the driver requests a full status refresh (in addition to the real-time push updates the fan sends on its own).\par
- Log Mode: driver debug logging destination.\par
\par
\b Notes / Limitations\b0\par
- This protocol is unofficial and undocumented by the manufacturer; it was derived from public network-traffic analysis by third parties (see driver.lua header for references). Firmware updates to the fan could change behavior.\par
- Occupancy/motion sensor state is not currently exposed.\par
- Verify the exact FAN / LIGHT_V2 proxy command and notification names in DriverEditor for your installed OS version before deploying to a live system; these are implemented per Control4's standard conventions but DriverEditor's autocomplete against your local proxy definitions is the authoritative source.\par
